Step 2: Examine the Repository Contents

Navigate into the cloned directory and check what's inside:
bash

cd challenge
ls -la
cat README.md

README.md contains:
text

# MyGit

### If you want the flag, make sure to push the flag!

Only flag.txt pushed by ```root:root@picoctf``` will be updated with the flag.

GOOD LUCK!

This tells us the server has a custom hook that will only provide the flag if we push a file named flag.txt with the author specified as root:root@picoctf (which translates to name="root", email="root@picoctf").
Step 3: Create a Placeholder Flag File

Create a dummy flag.txt file to push:
bash

echo "placeholder" > flag.txt

Step 4: Stage the File

Add the file to Git's staging area:
bash

git add flag.txt

Step 5: Check the Current Branch

Determine which branch we're working with:
bash

git branch

Output:
text

* master

The repository uses the master branch (not main).
Step 6: Configure Git User Identity

Git requires a committer identity even when we override the author. Set local repository configuration:
bash

git config user.email "temp@example.com"
git config user.name "temp"

Step 7: Commit with the Required Author

Commit the file using the --author flag to specify the exact author the server expects:
bash

git commit --author="root <root@picoctf>" -m "Add flag file"

Output:
text

[master d3040fe] Add flag file
 Author: root <root@picoctf>
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
 create mode 100644 flag.txt

Step 8: Push to the Repository

Push the commit to the remote repository on the master branch:
bash

git push origin master

